* text=auto
*.java text eol=lf
*.kt text eol=lf
# Everything under src/main/resources ships inside the jar, so its bytes are part of
# the artifact. Without pinning these, git checks them out as CRLF on Windows and the
# same sources build a jar with a different SHA every time the working copy is
# re-checked-out -- which makes "did this build actually change?" unanswerable from the
# hash alone. .json was pinned first and left ten other resource files (shaders, toml,
# mcmeta) still exposed; this covers the whole tree.
# The index already stores all of these as LF (text=auto normalised them), so pinning
# the working copy to LF changes no tracked content.
*.json text eol=lf
*.vsh text eol=lf
*.fsh text eol=lf
*.glsl text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.mcmeta text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.cfg text eol=lf
*.properties text eol=lf
*.txt text eol=lf
native/live2d/** text eol=lf
